      "title": "Why full body?",
      "topic": "4. Training frequency per muscle and split choice",
      "summary": "If total weekly hard sets are the same, training a muscle once, twice, or three times a week builds similar muscle. Training a muscle about twice a week is a practical way to spread sets so sessions stay manageable; growth is similar when weekly hard sets are equal. That's why full-body, upper/lower, and push/pull/legs splits are all good choices. A once-a-week \"bro split\" works too, but it crams a lot of sets into one session.",
      "recommendation": "Hit each muscle about 2× per week. Pick full body for 2–3 days a week, upper/lower for 4, and PPL for 5–6. A 5-day upper/lower + PPL hybrid (Upper, Lower, Push, Pull, Legs) also trains each muscle about twice a week. Only choose a bro split if you prefer it and keep the sets per session reasonable.",
      "evidenceStrength": "Mixed across meta-analyses, but they agree overall. The 2016 analysis favored 2×/week over 1×. The larger 2019 analysis found that frequency doesn't meaningfully matter once volume is equated. Pelland 2026 found frequency effects on hypertrophy could be negligible, but more frequency did help strength. So \"2× per week\" is a practical way to spread volume, not a growth requirement in itself.",
